{"step_index":6195,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T01:11:26Z","thinking":"The logging function's `self.logger` was configured to use `backend.add_log`. This implies server logs are being intermixed with the main application output stream. This needs to be addressed for clarity.\n\n\nA dedicated server logging mechanism is required. Implement a buffer within the server manager to store individual server logs with timestamps and names. These logs will be relayed to the backend, which will then dispatch them to the user interface via a custom event. The UI will then render these server logs in a dedicated terminal section.\n\n\nA reliable server shutdown mechanism is required, even when running as an administrator or externally. The initial approach involves an HTTP `/shutdown` endpoint to trigger a controlled exit within the server process. This will be implemented in both application instances. The server will call an `os._exit(0)` to exit.\n\n\nThe use of `os._exit(0)` within the server bypasses standard shutdown procedures, potentially causing issues with resource cleanup and permission checks, especially on Windows. The `server_manager.py`'s `stop()` method attempts multiple shutdown strategies: HTTP shutdown, process killing (via `taskkill` or `os.killpg`), and port-based process termination. Analysis reveals that the HTTP shutdown attempts to contact the shutdown endpoint first. Should this fail, the server process is aggressively terminated, and finally any leftover port listeners are killed.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ForEach-Object { Stop-Process -Id $_.OwningProcess -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }\"","Cwd":"\"D:\\\\AI_Engineer\\\\Pharmacy-agy\"","IsDaemon":"false","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Stopping processes on port 8000\"","toolSummary":"\"Stop-Process on port 8000\""}}]}
